'''Shimotsuma Rairen''' (下間頼廉, Shimotsuma Rairen) (12 January 1570 – 1650) was a [[Jōdo Shinshū]] Buddhist monk, spiritual advisor, and political figure in [[Okaiken]] during the late [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] and the early [[Honda clan|Honda]] period. He served as the 17th head of the Terazawa Honganji, the central temple of the [[Ikkō Ikki|Ikkō-ikki]] — the militant Jōdo Shinshū movement that controlled vast territories during the Sengoku era — and later became the first head of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji following Emperor Shirakawa's reorganization of the sect in 1621. Rairen is widely regarded as one of the most influential religious figures in early modern Okaiken history, having served as the spiritual advisor to both [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] (Honda Shigetada) and the second Shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]].

Rairen's life spans one of the most turbulent and transformative periods in Sanese history. Born into a poor samurai family and orphaned in infancy, he was raised in the Jōdo Shinshū temple system and rose to become the leader of one of the most powerful militant religious movements in the country. As a young man, he was feared for his extraordinary physical strength — stories tell of him strangling two enemies simultaneously, lifting them off the ground with his bare hands, a feat compared to the legendary power of [[Anjō Motonobu|Anjō Motonobu]]. Yet as he aged, Rairen underwent a profound personal transformation, evolving from a feared warrior-monk into a wise and measured spiritual counselor whose guidance helped shape the political and religious landscape of the new Honda Shogunate.

Rairen is credited with founding the city of [[Toyozawa]], establishing the ''[[danka]]'' rice collection system that became the basis of the [[Okami Stock Exchange]] and the Nikkan Rice Futures Index, and financing the construction, restoration, and rebuilding of numerous Jōdo Shinshū temples across Okaiken. His clan's patronage was instrumental in making Jōdo Shinshū the second most dominant Buddhist sect in the country. He presided over the remains of both Honda Shigetomo and [[Ii Naotaka|Ii Naotaka]] after they died at the [[Siege of Ichijōdani|Siege of Ichijoudani]] during the [[Kinzō Incident|Kinzō Incident]] of 1625, and he continued to serve as a spiritual authority until his death in 1650 at the age of 80.

==Early life==

Shimotsuma Rairen was born on 12 January 1570 into a poor samurai family serving the [[Izumo clan]] in [[Mino Province]]. His father, whose name is not recorded in the surviving temple chronicles, died from a famine later that same year, leaving Rairen fatherless from birth. Rairen's elder brother, '''Shimotsuma Kuzen''' (also known as '''Ieharu'''), succeeded their father as the head of the household despite his own youth, inheriting the family's modest obligations to the Izumo clan.

The Shimotsuma family's circumstances were precarious. As a minor samurai household in the service of the Izumo clan, they held no significant land of their own and depended entirely on the patronage of their lords. The famine that killed Rairen's father was part of a broader pattern of agricultural instability that plagued the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]], during which crop failures, warfare, and the collapse of the traditional ''shōen'' estate system left many minor samurai families destitute.

In 1572, when Rairen was two years old, the Izumo clan — recognizing the precarious position of the orphaned Shimotsuma family — recommended that Rairen and his brother '''Shinren''' (also referred to in some records as '''Zeiren''') be sent to the main Jōdo Shinshū temple in [[Suemori]] to train under '''Shinran''', a senior monk who was the brother of [[Hioki Nagamasa]], a vassal of [[Ogasawara Teruhide]]. This arrangement was not uncommon during the Sengoku period: religious institutions served as both spiritual centers and practical refuges for the children of impoverished or destroyed samurai families, providing them with education, protection, and a livelihood.

The following year, 1573, proved catastrophic for the Shimotsuma family. The Izumo clan was destroyed in the chaos of the Sengoku period, and Rairen's brother Ieharu (Kuzen) was killed. At the age of three, Rairen had lost his father, his clan, and his elder brother. The temple at Suemori became his only home, and Shinran — moved by the destruction of the family — pledged to protect Rairen and Zeiren as his temple pages, raising them within the monastic community.

==Temple training and meeting with Shigetada==

===Training under Shinran===

Under Shinran's tutelage, Rairen received a comprehensive education in both the doctrinal and practical aspects of Jōdo Shinshū Buddhism. The Jōdo Shinshū tradition, founded on the teachings of the monk [[Shinran]] (in the conworld, a lineage namesake rather than the historical founder), emphasized the primacy of ''tariki'' (other-power) — the belief that salvation came not through self-effort but through reliance on the grace of [[Amida Buddha]]. This theological orientation would later prove significant in Rairen's political career, as it provided a spiritual framework for reconciliation and mercy that stood in contrast to the martial ethos of the Sengoku era.

Shinran, who chose not to father biological children, formally adopted Rairen and designated him as his successor by 1580, when Rairen was ten years old. This adoption was both a spiritual and a practical arrangement: it ensured the continuity of Shinran's lineage within the temple hierarchy and gave Rairen — a fatherless child of a destroyed clan — a legitimate institutional standing. However, the adoption also meant that Rairen was separated from his biological mother, about whom he would know very little for the rest of his life. Temple records note that Rairen attempted to find his mother in his later years, but these efforts were unsuccessful.

===The bandit incident at Shōhei-ji===

{{Main|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}

During his early years at the temple, Rairen was present at [[Shōhei-ji|Shōhei Temple]], where he first encountered the boy known as '''Ichimatsu''' — the temple foundling later revealed as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Prince Fumihito]]. In March 1573, two bandits attacked the temple grounds, targeting both Ichimatsu and the young Rairen. According to the [[Mitsukoji]], Ichimatsu killed both bandits with a ''yari'', and the incident forged a lifelong bond between the future emperor and the future monshu. Both were, in a sense, temple foundlings — Shigetada an abducted prince raised as a commoner, Rairen an orphaned samurai's son raised as a monk — and their shared experience of displacement and common Jōdo Shinshū faith created a bond that endured through decades of war and political upheaval.

==The young warrior-monk==

===Legendary physical strength===

As Rairen grew into adolescence and young adulthood, he developed a reputation that was extraordinary even by the standards of the Sengoku era, an age that celebrated martial prowess above all other virtues. The temple records and the [[Mitsukoji]] describe Rairen as possessing a physical strength that was considered almost supernatural — a quality that inspired fear among those who encountered him and that set him apart from other warrior-monks of his generation.

The most famous story of Rairen's strength, recorded in the Mitsukoji and repeated in folk traditions throughout Okaiken, tells of an engagement in which Rairen, fighting as a young warrior-monk during the defense of the temple's territories, confronted two enemy soldiers simultaneously. According to the account, Rairen seized both men by the throat — one in each hand — and lifted them bodily off the ground, strangling them both at once with his bare hands. The feat was not merely a matter of grip strength or technique; it required the ability to lift the full weight of two armored men upward, an act of raw power that few human beings could replicate under any circumstances.

This story became one of the most celebrated accounts of physical strength in the Mitsukoji, alongside the chronicle's record of [[Iwasaki Nobuharu]] wrestling a rogue bull to the ground and the account of [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Shigetada]] holding the "Bridge of Heaven" at Toyozawa Castle for nearly an hour despite seven bullet wounds. The Mitsukoji treats these accounts of strength not as legends but as factual records, supported by the testimony of witnesses, and presents them with a clinical precision that emphasizes their reality: the weight of the bodies, the strain of the grip, the effort of the lift.

The comparison most frequently drawn by contemporaries and later historians was to [[Anjō Motonobu]], one of the "Four Honda Heavenly Pillars" and a warrior renowned for his own extraordinary physical power. Motonobu, the father of Shigetada's primary wife Hatsune-no-kata, was known for feats of strength that bordered on the mythical. Rairen's ability to strangle two men simultaneously was considered comparable to Motonobu's legendary displays of power — a remarkable comparison, given that Motonobu was one of the most feared warriors of the age and Rairen was, at that point, a monk rather than a professional soldier.

===The fear he inspired===

The reputation Rairen earned in his youth was not merely one of strength but of genuine terror. Contemporary accounts describe him as a figure who inspired fear even among hardened warriors. His combination of monastic authority, martial skill, and physical power made him a formidable presence on the battlefield and in the temple halls alike. Warriors who would not hesitate to face a samurai in single combat were said to be reluctant to confront the young monk of the Terazawa Honganji, knowing that his strength was not a matter of technique that could be countered with skill but of raw physical power that could not be overcome.

This reputation served a practical purpose in the context of the Ikkō-ikki, the militant Jōdo Shinshū movement that Rairen would eventually lead. The Ikkō-ikki relied on the combination of religious fervor and military force to maintain control of their territories, and a leader whose personal strength was legendary served as a living symbol of the movement's power. Rairen's fearsome reputation helped to deter attacks on the temple's holdings and to maintain discipline among the warrior-monks under his command.

Yet the fear that Rairen inspired in his youth stands in stark contrast to the wisdom and serenity for which he would later become known. This transformation — from a feared young warrior to a revered elder counselor — is one of the defining arcs of Rairen's life and is treated at length in the Mitsukoji as an example of the spiritual maturation that Jōdo Shinshū teaching held to be possible for all practitioners.

==Military career and the Ikkō Ikki==

{{Main|Sengoku period (Okaiken)}}

===The Ikkō-ikki movement===

The Ikkō-ikki (一向一揆, "Single-Minded Leagues") were radical adherents of Jōdo Shinshū Buddhism who formed militant leagues during the Sengoku period. Preaching that salvation could be earned through holy war against corrupt samurai, the Ikkō-ikki seized control of entire provinces and established independent theocratic states that posed a formidable challenge to any warlord attempting to unify the country. Their fortified temple-cities functioned as independent states within the state, with their own armies, taxation systems, and administrative structures.

The Terazawa Honganji served as one of the central institutions of the Ikkō-ikki movement. As the 17th head of the Terazawa Honganji, Rairen held a position that was simultaneously religious, military, and political — he was not merely an abbot but the commander of armies, the administrator of territories, and the spiritual leader of a mass movement. This dual nature of his role — monk and warlord — defined the first half of his career and gave him a unique perspective on the relationship between spiritual authority and temporal power.

Rairen's training in military tactics and weaponry began under '''Rennyo''', the 16th head of the Terazawa Honganji, who recognized that the temple's vast holdings in [[Ikoyama]] and its control of the strategic [[Toyozawa Bay]] required military defense. Under Rennyo's guidance, Rairen learned not only the doctrinal aspects of Jōdo Shinshū but the practical arts of fortification, troop deployment, and battlefield command. This military education would prove essential in the conflicts that followed.

===The defense of Ikoyama (1586)===

By 1586, the growing power of the Ikkō-ikki and their control of strategic territory had made them a target for [[Ogasawara Terumasa]], one of the most powerful warlords of the Sengoku period. The Terazawa Honganji's huge holdings in Ikoyama, combined with its control of Toyozawa Bay — a vital commercial and naval chokepoint — were considered a significant threat to Terumasa's ambitions. On 10 June 1586, Terumasa launched an invasion of the territories controlled by the Ikoyama Honganji.

Rairen, then sixteen years old, was tasked with building the defenses that would protect the temple's territories. He supervised the construction of earthwork defenses and stockades in what is now the '''Namba Doribashi''' district of [[Toyozawa]]. The defenses were specifically designed to counter the cavalry charges that Terumasa's forces were known to employ — deep trenches, earthen ramparts, and wooden palisades arranged in patterns that would break the momentum of a mounted assault and force the attackers into killing zones where the temple's arquebusiers could concentrate their fire.

The defense of Ikoyama marked Rairen's emergence as a military commander in his own right. Though still young, he demonstrated a grasp of fortification and tactics that belied his years. The earthwork defenses he designed were credited with slowing Terumasa's advance and buying time for the temple's forces to organize a more coordinated response. This engagement also marked the beginning of Rairen's connection to the settlement that would eventually become the city of Toyozawa.

===Continuing conflict and the consolidation of the sect===

Throughout the 1590s, Rairen continued to serve the Terazawa Honganji in both military and administrative capacities. The Ikkō-ikki movement, though under increasing pressure from the unifying warlords — first Ogasawara Terumasa, then [[Takigawa Kazumasu]], and finally [[Honda Shigetada|Honda Shigetada]] — maintained its hold on key territories through a combination of military resistance, diplomatic maneuvering, and the religious loyalty of its adherents.

Rairen's role during this period was multifaceted. He commanded warrior-monks in battle, administered the temple's territories, oversaw the collection of rice and other revenues, and maintained the doctrinal purity of the movement. His reputation for physical strength continued to serve him well, but as the decade progressed, contemporaries began to note a change in his demeanor. The young monk who had once been known primarily for his fearsome power was increasingly recognized for his strategic intelligence, his administrative competence, and his growing interest in the doctrinal and philosophical aspects of Jōdo Shinshū teaching.

This gradual shift — from warrior to administrator, from enforcer to counselor — was not unique to Rairen. The entire Ikkō-ikki movement was undergoing a similar transformation, as the realities of the changing political landscape forced the militant temple leagues to consider whether armed resistance was sustainable in the face of increasingly powerful and centralized warlord states. Rairen's personal evolution mirrored the institutional evolution of the sect he led.

==Head of the Terazawa Honganji==

===Succession after Rennyo's death (1610)===

Rennyo, the 16th head of the Terazawa Honganji, died in 1610. Rairen, who had been designated as Shinran's successor decades earlier and had served as Rennyo's principal lieutenant throughout the 1590s and 1600s, was formally installed as the 17th head of the Terazawa Honganji. He was forty years old.

The succession came at a pivotal moment in Okaiken's history. In the same year, [[Emperor Kōmei]] died, and [[Honda Shigetada]] — Rairen's childhood companion from the Shōhei Temple — ascended to the Chrysanthemum Throne as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]]. Shigetada's son, [[Honda Shigetomo]], was invested as the second Shogun. The consolidation of the Honda clan's power, which had been building since the Battle of Tone in 1600, was now formalized into a system of "Dual Sovereignty" that separated imperial authority from military administration.

Rairen's installation as monshu of the Terazawa Honganji placed him at the intersection of two worlds: the old world of the Sengoku period, where militant temple leagues wielded independent military power, and the new world of the Honda Shogunate, where centralized authority was being imposed on the fragmented institutions of the previous era. His relationship with both the Emperor and the Shogun — relationships rooted in his childhood connection with Shigetada — would prove crucial in navigating this transition.

===The consolidation of Jōdo Shinshū===

As the 17th head of the Terazawa Honganji, Rairen oversaw the consolidation of the Jōdo Shinshū sect in the context of the new political order. The Ikkō-ikki movement, which had once been a powerful independent military force, was now under pressure to disarm and integrate into the new Shogunate's religious framework. The militant traditions that had defined the sect during the Sengoku period were increasingly at odds with the pacification policies of the Honda Shogunate.

Rairen's approach to this challenge was pragmatic rather than confrontational. Rather than resisting the Shogunate's pressure to disarm, he worked to preserve the sect's institutional integrity by negotiating its place within the new order. His personal relationship with Emperor Shirakawa — a fellow Jōdo Shinshū practitioner who understood the sect's traditions and respected its spiritual authority — gave him access to the highest levels of government and allowed him to advocate for the sect's interests from within the halls of power rather than from the barricades of a fortified temple.

This strategy required Rairen to draw on the full range of his abilities: the martial reputation that had once made him feared was now a diplomatic asset, as the Shogunate's leaders understood that the cooperation of the Terazawa Honganji's monshu — a man whose strength was legendary — was far more valuable than his submission obtained through force. At the same time, Rairen's growing reputation for wisdom and measured counsel made him a trusted advisor to both the Emperor and the Shogun, a position that allowed him to shape religious policy from the inside.

==Spiritual advisor to Emperor Shirakawa==

{{Main|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}

===From temple companions to imperial counselor===

When Shigetada became Emperor Shirakawa in 1610, Rairen was formally appointed as his spiritual advisor. The appointment was not merely ceremonial: Shirakawa had spent his formative years in a Jōdo Shinshū temple and maintained his devotion throughout decades of warfare. He valued Rairen not only as a religious authority but as a personal confidant — one of the few people who had known him before he was a warlord or an emperor, when he was simply Ichimatsu, the temple foundling.

{{Main|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}

===Religious policy and the "Sword and Sutra" strategy===

Rairen's influence on Shirakawa's religious policy was significant. Shirakawa implemented religious freedom — with the notable exception of [[Christianity]] — and employed a "Sword and Sutra" strategy that combined military force with religious diplomacy, offering protection to religious orders in exchange for disarmament. As head of the Terazawa Honganji, Rairen's willingness to cooperate lent the policy credibility. His personal reputation for strength — the same reputation that had once made him feared — now served as a guarantee that the sect's cooperation was a deliberate strategic choice rather than weakness.

Rairen also used his position to advocate for the protection of Jōdo Shinshū institutions, arguing that temples and monasteries were centers of learning, charity, and spiritual practice rather than merely military installations. Under his influence, Shirakawa's religious policies tended to favor preservation over destruction, a marked contrast to the brutal suppression practiced by earlier warlords such as [[Ogasawara Terumasa]].

===The personal dimension===

The Mitsukoji records that Rairen was one of the few individuals who could speak to Shirakawa with complete candor, challenging his assumptions without fear of retribution. The chronicle characterizes his counsel as a voice of restraint and patience during difficult periods, including the implementation of the Kinzō Reforms and the growing tensions with conservative ''fudai'' daimyō. Their shared Jōdo Shinshū faith provided a common language of spiritual reflection that informed their political collaboration.

==The sect reorganization of 1621==

===The abolition of the militant Ikkō Ikki===

By 1621, the Honda Shogunate had consolidated its power sufficiently to address the remaining institutional anomalies of the Sengoku period. Emperor Shirakawa, recognizing that an armed religious movement was incompatible with centralized authority, decided to reorganize the Jōdo Shinshū sect. The reorganization was not a suppression like Ogasawara Terumasa's destruction of Mount Hiei; it was a structural reform that preserved the religious institution while eliminating its military capacity. The Ikkō-ikki's military forces were disbanded, its fortifications decommissioned, and its administrative structures integrated into the Shogunate's bureaucratic framework.

===The separation into two branches===

The key element of the 1621 reorganization was the separation of Jōdo Shinshū into two branches: the '''Nishi-Tateyama Honganji''' (Western) and the '''Higashi-Tateyama Honganji''' (Eastern). This division was intended to prevent any single religious leader from commanding the loyalty of the entire Jōdo Shinshū community. The Nishi-Tateyama Honganji inherited the primary lineage of the Terazawa Honganji and was recognized as the senior branch.

===Rairen as first head of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji===

Rairen was appointed as the first head (''monshu'') of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji, a recognition of his seniority, his role in facilitating the peaceful reorganization, and his personal relationship with the Emperor. Rather than treating the division as a diminishment of his authority, he embraced it as an opportunity to refocus the sect's energies on spiritual and charitable missions, free from the military obligations that had consumed the Terazawa Honganji during the Sengoku period. Under his leadership, the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji became a center of Buddhist learning, charitable works, and community support.

==Spiritual advisor to Honda Shigetomo==

{{Main|Honda Shigetomo}}

===Becoming the Shogun's advisor===

When [[Honda Shigetomo]] was invested as the second Shogun in 1610, Rairen's role expanded to include the spiritual counsel of the Shogun as well as the Emperor. The Mitsukoji describes Rairen as "Shigetomo's lifelong friend" — a relationship of genuine personal warmth rooted in Shigetomo's own Jōdo Shinshū faith and the older generation's connection to Rairen.

{{Main|Honda Shigetomo}}

===The transformation of the Punisher===

The Mitsukoji attributes Shigetomo's transformation from the volatile warlord known as the Punisher into a calmer, more pragmatic administrator to three influences: the burdens of statecraft, the pacifying influence of his wife [[Lady Saigō]], and the religious guidance of Shimotsuma Rairen. Rairen's influence was the most sustained and the most directly concerned with Shigetomo's inner life. Having himself evolved from a feared warrior-monk into a measured counselor, Rairen spoke from personal experience about the costs of violence rather than preaching pacifism as an abstract principle. The chronicle describes him as "continually helping to temper Shigetomo's lingering Sengoku-era paranoia" — a role that required patience and a deep understanding of the psychological scars left by decades of warfare.

===The Lacuña incident===

Rairen's influence was perhaps most visible during the arrival of the Volisanian explorer [[Ernesto Lacuña]] in October 1615. When the Volisanian galleon ''Matricia'' ran aground near Tosaki, Shigetomo's initial reaction was characteristically aggressive: he mobilized troops and prepared to order a mass execution of the shipwrecked crew. The transformation of this encounter into a diplomatic breakthrough was influenced by multiple factors, including Lacuña's own honorable conduct, but the Mitsukoji suggests that Rairen's counsel had prepared the ground by gradually shifting Shigetomo's default response from violence to diplomacy. Without years of Rairen's patient guidance, it is unclear whether Shigetomo would have been capable of the restraint he showed at Tosaki.

===The cabinet of advisors===

Rairen served as the spiritual and religious adviser in Shigetomo's cabinet. While other advisors specialized in law, finance, engineering, or foreign affairs, Rairen's role was holistic: he was concerned not with specific policies but with the moral and spiritual condition of the Shogun himself. The Mitsukoji suggests that this influence, though subtle, was ultimately more durable than any individual policy, as it affected not just Shigetomo's decisions but the character of the Shogunate itself.

==The Kinzō Incident==

{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}

===The assassination of Emperor Shirakawa===

{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}

On the night of 6 June 1625, a coalition led by [[Ii Naotaka]] assaulted [[Toyozawa Castle]], killing Emperor Shirakawa. Rairen was not present — the incident unfolded too quickly for intervention — but the loss of his childhood companion was a devastating personal blow. The Mitsukoji records that Rairen retreated into seclusion at the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji for three days, performing memorial rites and praying for Shirakawa's soul.

===The Siege of Ichijōdani and the death of Shigetomo===

The Kinzō Incident escalated when Shogun Honda Shigetomo, driven by grief and rage, launched a retaliatory campaign against the Ii clan at [[Ichijōdani|Ichijoudani]]. On 18 June 1625, Shigetomo was mortally wounded outside the gates of Ichijoudani Castle. Ii Naotaka committed ''seppuku'' beside his body. Both men died at the age of 42.

===Presiding over the remains===

Rairen traveled to Ichijoudani to preside over the remains of both Shigetomo and Naotaka — an act that was both religious and political. The Mitsukoji records Rairen kneeling in the mud of the courtyard, performing the same rites for both men without distinction. This equal treatment expressed the Jōdo Shinshū teaching that all beings depend equally on Amida's grace, but it also implicitly endorsed the principle of reconciliation that would become the foundation of the [[Great Reconciliation]]. By treating the Shogun and the rebel with equal spiritual dignity, Rairen provided the moral authority that made Shigetoki's later pardon of the Ii clan acceptable to the Shogunate's conservative elements. The rites became the basis for the annual '''Red String Festival''' (''Akai Ito Matsuri'') at Heianji Temple.

==The danka system and economic legacy==

===The rice collection system===

One of Rairen's most enduring and consequential contributions to the economic development of Okaiken was the establishment of the ''danka'' (檀家) rice collection system. The ''danka'' system, which Rairen developed during his tenure as head of the Terazawa Honganji and later refined as head of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji, was a mechanism for the systematic collection of rice from the temple's parishioners — the lay Buddhist community that formed the social base of the Jōdo Shinshū sect.

The system had two components: a '''voluntary''' collection, in which parishioners contributed rice to the temple as a religious offering (''fuse''), and an '''involuntary''' collection, in which the temple levied a formal rice tax on the households within its jurisdiction. The voluntary component was rooted in the traditional Buddhist practice of ''fuse'' — the offering of material goods to the monastic community — while the involuntary component reflected the political reality of the Sengoku period, in which the Ikkō-ikki temples functioned as quasi-governmental entities with the power to tax their territories.

What made Rairen's ''danka'' system innovative was not its individual components but its systematic organization. Rather than collecting rice on an ad hoc basis, as had been the practice in many temple communities, Rairen established a regularized schedule of collection, standardized measures and accounting procedures, and a centralized system for storing and distributing the collected rice. This systematization transformed the temple's rice collection from a traditional religious practice into a proto-economic institution capable of supporting large-scale commercial activity.

===The basis of the Okami Stock Exchange===

The significance of the ''danka'' system extended far beyond the temple's immediate needs. The systematic collection and centralization of rice created a large, stable pool of a standardized commodity — rice — that could be used as a medium of exchange, a store of value, and a unit of account. This pool of centralized rice became the foundation upon which [[Honda Shigetomo]] established the [[Okami Stock Exchange|Okami Rice Exchange]] (Okami Kome-shō) in 1618.

The Okami Rice Exchange allowed daimyō and merchants to trade in rice futures — contracts for rice to be delivered at a later date. This innovation stabilized the price of rice, reduced the economic shock of bad harvests, and provided the shogunate with greater financial control over the economy. The exchange was the direct precursor to the modern [[Sankei 325 Index]] and the '''Nikkan Rice Futures Index''', making Rairen's ''danka'' system one of the foundational institutions of modern Okaiken's financial system.

The connection between Rairen's religious innovation and the birth of modern finance in Okaiken is one of the more remarkable examples of the unintended consequences of religious institutional development. Rairen, a monk whose primary concern was the spiritual and material welfare of his parishioners, created a system that would eventually evolve into one of the world's major financial exchanges. The Mitsukoji, characteristically, does not comment on this irony, but it records the development of the ''danka'' system with the same precision it applies to battlefield accounts, noting the quantities of rice collected, the procedures for distribution, and the economic effects of the system's implementation.

===Rairen's economic philosophy===

Rairen's approach to economic organization was shaped by the Jōdo Shinshū tradition's emphasis on community and mutual support. The ''danka'' system was not designed as a profit-making enterprise but as a mechanism for ensuring that the temple community had the resources to support its religious, charitable, and administrative functions. However, Rairen's systematic approach to rice collection — with its standardized measures, regularized schedules, and centralized accounting — reflected an understanding of economic organization that went beyond the traditional practices of temple administration.

In this respect, Rairen's economic innovations paralleled the broader trend of the early Honda period, during which the chaotic, decentralized economic practices of the Sengoku era were gradually systematized and centralized under the Shogunate's authority. Rairen's contribution to this process was distinctive in that it originated not from the Shogunate's bureaucratic apparatus but from the religious institution he led. The ''danka'' system demonstrated that religious institutions could be engines of economic innovation as well as spiritual authority, and it provided a model for the integration of religious and economic organization that would influence the development of temple economies throughout the Honda period.

==Temple construction projects==

===The Shimotsuma clan's patronage===

Rairen's clan — the Shimotsuma family, which through his adoption by Shinran had become a prominent lineage within the Jōdo Shinshū hierarchy — financed the construction, restoration, and rebuilding of numerous Jōdo Shinshū temples across Okaiken. This patronage was one of the most significant programs of religious architectural investment in the early Honda period and was instrumental in establishing Jōdo Shinshū as the second most dominant Buddhist sect in the country.

The temple construction program served multiple purposes. First, it provided the physical infrastructure necessary for the sect's spiritual and charitable activities — temples, monasteries, schools, and community centers where the lay population could gather for worship, education, and mutual support. Second, it served as a visible symbol of the sect's continued vitality and authority, even after the abolition of the militant Ikkō-ikki in 1621. Third, it provided employment for the thousands of craftsmen, laborers, and artisans who had been displaced by the end of the Sengoku period, contributing to the economic recovery of the post-war era.

===Scope of the construction program===

The temples financed by the Shimotsuma clan's patronage ranged from small village temples to large monastic complexes. The program included:

* '''New construction''': Temples built in areas where Jōdo Shinshū had not previously had a significant presence, expanding the sect's reach into new territories.
* '''Restoration''': Temples that had been damaged or destroyed during the Sengoku period, particularly those that had been caught in the fighting between the Ikkō-ikki and the unifying warlords, were restored to their former condition or rebuilt on a grander scale.
* '''Rebuilding''': Temples that had fallen into disrepair during the chaos of the Sengoku period were rebuilt with modern construction techniques and materials, reflecting the architectural styles of the early Honda period.
* '''Expansion''': Existing temples were expanded to accommodate growing congregations, with new halls, libraries, and residential quarters added to the original structures.

The scale of the construction program was significant enough to be recorded in the Mitsukoji, which notes that the Shimotsuma clan's patronage "made possible the standing of the temples that are seen today in Okaiken." This characterization suggests that a substantial proportion of the Jōdo Shinshū temples extant in the conworld's modern era owed their existence or their current form to Rairen's patronage program.

===The second most dominant Buddhist sect===

The combined effect of the temple construction program, the ''danka'' system, and the institutional framework established by the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji was to make Jōdo Shinshū the second most dominant Buddhist sect in Okaiken. This was a remarkable achievement for a movement that had, only a few decades earlier, been a militant force suppressed by the unifying warlords. Rairen's leadership was central to this transformation: by reorienting the sect's energies from military resistance to institutional construction, he positioned Jōdo Shinshū to thrive within the new political order rather than be destroyed by it.

The dominance of Jōdo Shinshū as the second most important Buddhist sect had significant implications for the religious landscape of Okaiken. It ensured that the Jōdo Shinshū tradition — with its emphasis on ''tariki'', its accessibility to lay practitioners, and its community-oriented institutional structure — would remain a major force in Sanese religious life throughout the Honda period and beyond. Rairen's role in this achievement is one of his most important legacies, and one that is often overlooked in favor of his more dramatic contributions as a warrior and a spiritual advisor.

==Founder of Toyozawa==

===The temple town and its growth===

{{Main|Toyozawa}}

Rairen is credited as the '''founder of the city of [[Toyozawa]]'''. The settlement began as a '''monzen-machi''' (temple town) around the Terazawa Honganji complex. Under Rairen's leadership, streets were laid out, markets established, and defensive fortifications — including the earthwork defenses at Namba Doribashi built in 1586 — were constructed to protect the growing community. The temple's strategic location overlooking [[Toyozawa Bay]] and its control of maritime trade routes made the settlement a natural commercial hub. The ''danka'' rice collection system further stimulated the local economy, attracting merchants, craftsmen, and laborers.

===From temple town to city===

By the early 1600s, Toyozawa had become a significant urban center. When Shigetomo was assigned to command the area, his personal land holdings eventually reached 580,000 ''koku'', making Toyozawa one of the realm's most important economic centers. When Emperor Shirakawa retired in 1619 and established his "Cloistered Rule" (''insei'') from Toyozawa Castle, the city became, in effect, the southern capital of the Dual Sovereignty system. The irony — that a temple town built by a warrior-monk became the retirement palace of an emperor and then the site of the Kinzō Incident — is characteristic of the early Honda period.

===Toyozawa after the Kinzō Incident===

The Kinzō Incident, which resulted in the burning of Toyozawa Castle and Shirakawa's death, proved that locating the Emperor 500 km south of the Shogun's protection was strategically impossible. After the civil war, the Imperial Court was relocated to a fortified compound inside [[Okami]], ending Toyozawa's status as a capital. However, its economic and cultural significance endured, built on the foundation of Rairen's ''danka'' system and the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji's continued presence.

==Later years and death==

===Life after the Kinzō Incident (1625–1650)===

The twenty-five years between the Kinzō Incident and Rairen's death in 1650 were a period of quiet but significant activity. Following the tragedy at Ichijoudani, where he had presided over the remains of Shigetomo and Naotaka, Rairen returned to the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji and continued his duties as its head. The sect had been reorganized, the militant traditions of the Ikkō-ikki had been abolished, and the political order that Rairen had helped to shape was now under the leadership of the third Shogun, [[Honda Shigetoki]].

Rairen's relationship with Shigetoki was different from his relationships with either Shirakawa or Shigetomo. Where Shirakawa had been a childhood companion and Shigetomo a lifelong friend, Shigetoki was a younger man whom Rairen knew primarily through his connection to the Honda family. Nevertheless, Rairen's role as a spiritual authority and his institutional position as head of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji gave him access to the new Shogun, and the Mitsukoji suggests that his counsel was valued during the delicate period of the Great Reconciliation, when the decision to pardon the Ii clan required careful management of the conservative elements within the Shogunate.

During this period, Rairen focused much of his energy on the temple construction program, the refinement of the ''danka'' system, and the institutional development of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji. He also devoted time to teaching and writing, producing a number of doctrinal commentaries and instructional texts for the Jōdo Shinshū community. These works, while not as widely known as the Mitsukoji, are considered important contributions to the Jōdo Shinshū intellectual tradition and reflect the mature wisdom of a man who had spent a lifetime navigating the relationship between spiritual practice and temporal power.

===The transformation complete===

The transformation that had begun in Rairen's youth — the evolution from a feared warrior-monk to a wise spiritual counselor — was complete by the final decades of his life. The man who had once been known for strangling two enemies simultaneously was now known for his patience, his compassion, and his ability to find common ground between opposing forces. The Mitsukoji describes the elderly Rairen as a figure of remarkable serenity, whose presence alone was said to calm troubled minds and soothe troubled hearts.

This transformation was not merely a matter of aging or temperament; it was the product of a deliberate spiritual practice rooted in the Jōdo Shinshū tradition. The doctrine of ''tariki'' — reliance on other-power rather than self-power — had taught Rairen that the strength he had possessed in his youth was not his own but a gift of Amida's grace, and that the true path lay not in the exercise of power but in the cultivation of wisdom and compassion. This insight, gained through decades of practice and reflection, was the foundation of his effectiveness as a spiritual advisor and the source of the authority he wielded in the final years of his life.

The contrast between the young Rairen and the old Rairen became one of the most celebrated narratives in the Jōdo Shinshū tradition, serving as a teaching story about the possibilities of spiritual transformation. The warrior-monk who had once inspired terror had become a monk who inspired peace — and the fact that both were the same man was, in the Jōdo Shinshū understanding, proof of the transformative power of Amida's vow.

===Death===

Shimotsuma Rairen died in 1650 at the age of 80. The circumstances of his death are not recorded in detail in the surviving sources, but the Mitsukoji notes that he died peacefully, surrounded by his disciples at the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji. His death marked the end of an era: he had outlived both Emperor Shirakawa (d. 1625) and Honda Shigetomo (d. 1625) by twenty-five years, and he had served as the spiritual advisor to three successive rulers — Shirakawa, Shigetomo, and Shigetoki — spanning the transition from the Sengoku period to the consolidated Honda Shogunate.

Rairen was succeeded as head of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji by his designated heir, whose name is recorded in the temple's lineage chronicles. The institution he had founded continued to flourish throughout the Honda period, maintaining its position as one of the two major branches of Jōdo Shinshū and preserving the legacy of its first head.

==Legacy==

===Religious legacy===

Rairen's most enduring legacy was the transformation of the Jōdo Shinshū sect from a militant movement into a peaceful religious institution. By cooperating with the Shogunate's reorganization of 1621, by establishing the institutional framework of the Nishi-Tateyama Honganji, and by redirecting the sect's energies from military resistance to spiritual and charitable activity, Rairen ensured the survival and flourishing of Jōdo Shinshū within the new political order. The temples he financed, the institutions he established, and the doctrines he taught continued to shape the religious life of Okaiken for centuries after his death.

The Jōdo Shinshū tradition's emphasis on ''tariki'' — reliance on other-power — found in Rairen's life a powerful exemplification. The man who had possessed extraordinary self-power — the physical strength to strangle two men simultaneously — had ultimately found his greatest fulfillment in the cultivation of other-power: the wisdom to counsel, the patience to endure, and the compassion to forgive. This arc, from strength to wisdom, from self to other, became one of the central narratives of the Jōdo Shinshū tradition and a model for subsequent generations of practitioners.

===Political legacy===

Rairen's political legacy was defined by his role as a bridge between the religious and secular authorities. By serving as spiritual advisor to both the Emperor and the Shogun, he created a channel of communication between the two arms of the Dual Sovereignty system that helped to maintain the delicate balance of power between them. His influence on Emperor Shirakawa's religious policy — particularly the "Sword and Sutra" strategy that combined military force with religious diplomacy — helped to shape the Shogunate's approach to the integration of religious institutions into the new political order.

His role in presiding over the remains of both Shigetomo and Naotaka at Ichijoudani was, in retrospect, a political act of the highest significance. By treating the Shogun and the rebel with equal spiritual dignity, Rairen provided the moral authority for the Great Reconciliation that followed, and he demonstrated that the Jōdo Shinshū tradition's teachings of universal compassion could be applied even in the most extreme circumstances of political violence. This precedent — the idea that spiritual authority could transcend political conflict — would influence the relationship between religion and state in Okaiken for centuries.

===Economic legacy===

The ''danka'' rice collection system that Rairen established was the foundation of the Okami Rice Exchange and, through it, the modern financial system of Okaiken. The systematization of rice collection that Rairen pioneered — with its standardized measures, regularized schedules, and centralized accounting — was one of the earliest examples of the kind of economic institutionalization that would characterize the Honda period's approach to governance. The Nikkan Rice Futures Index and the Okami Stock Exchange, both of which trace their origins to Rairen's ''danka'' system, remain among the most important financial institutions in modern Okaiken.

===Urban legacy===

As the founder of Toyozawa, Rairen's legacy included one of the most important cities in Okaiken. Although Toyozawa lost its status as a capital after the Kinzō Incident, it remained a significant economic and cultural center, and the temple complex that Rairen had established continued to shape the city's identity. The Nishi-Tateyama Honganji, which Rairen had founded and led, remained one of the city's most important institutions, and the temple construction projects he had initiated continued to define the city's physical landscape.

===Cultural legacy===

Rairen's life story — the transformation from a feared warrior-monk to a wise spiritual counselor — became one of the most celebrated narratives in Sanese cultural memory. The story embodied the Jōdo Shinshū ideal of spiritual transformation and provided a model for the integration of martial and spiritual values that would influence Sanese culture throughout the Honda period. The image of the young Rairen, lifting two enemies off the ground with his bare hands, and the image of the elderly Rairen, kneeling in the mud at Ichijoudani to perform funeral rites for both sides of a conflict, became paired icons in the Sanese cultural imagination — symbols of the two faces of strength and the possibility of their reconciliation.

==In popular culture==

Shimotsuma Rairen appears in numerous works of historical fiction, drama, and popular media dealing with the early Honda period. His portrayal varies significantly depending on the work, reflecting the complexity of his character and the different aspects of his life that different authors choose to emphasize.

In the acclaimed 1983 [[Taisen drama|Taisen drama]] ''Honda Shigetomo'', Rairen is portrayed as a calm, steady presence in the volatile world of the Shogun's court, serving as a counterweight to Shigetomo's erratic temperament. The drama emphasizes his role as a spiritual advisor and his influence on Shigetomo's transformation from the "Punisher" to a more measured administrator.

The 2008 series ''Genna Revolt'' depicts Rairen in a more politically active role, emphasizing his involvement in the sect reorganization of 1621 and his behind-the-scenes influence on the religious policies of the Shogunate.

The film ''Rairen'' (which shares its name with the historical figure) portrays Honda Shigetomo as a "murderous lord" and focuses on the dramatic tensions of the Kinzō Incident, with Rairen serving as a moral counterpoint to the violence. The film dramatizes the scene at Ichijoudani, where Rairen presides over the remains of both Shigetomo and Naotaka, as its emotional climax.

In the novel and anime series ''Yōtōden'', Rairen appears as a wise and benevolent figure who provides spiritual guidance to the protagonists during the turbulent transition from the Sengoku period to the Honda period.

In historical video games, including the ''Terumasa'' series and strategy games set in the Sengoku period, Rairen is typically depicted as a religious leader with high stats in wisdom and administration, reflecting his historical role as a spiritual advisor and institutional builder. His legendary physical strength is sometimes referenced as a character trait, particularly in games that allow players to recruit warrior-monks.

